.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap \- deploy and run applications on Euclid
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B eap
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B eap
module talks to the Euclid gateway's eap service to define applications -
programs Euclid runs, scales and routes requests to - and to start and
stop them.
.PP
An application can be written in any language. Euclid starts it, restarts
it if it dies, scales it between a minimum and maximum instance count,
and forwards requests addressed to it through the gateway. It links no
Euclid library and needs no Euclid header: all it has to do is listen on
the Unix socket named in its
.I EUCLID_SOCKET
environment variable and answer
.I x-euclid-action
requests on it, exactly as a Euclid module does. Creating that socket is
also how it reports that it started.
.PP
The artifact is an object in an
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
bucket rather than a path on a host, so deploying is an ordinary upload -
through the CLI, an SDK, or an FTP/SFTP transfer server. The manager
copies it to the host on first start, which is what lets a fresh host
bring an application up from nothing but the database and the object
store.
.PP
.B eap
itself never runs anything. It owns the application
.I definitions
only, and
.B start-application
and
.B stop-application
do nothing but record the state an application
.I should
be in. The Euclid manager notices within a few seconds and starts or
stops the processes. This is why every action returns immediately, and
why
.B list-applications
output carries both a
.I desiredState
and an observed
.IR state ,
alongside the number of instances actually running.
.PP
Because the definitions are stored rather than sent as commands, a
manager restart brings every application marked running back by itself,
and
.B eap
being unreachable at the moment a change is requested does not lose it.
Changing a definition restarts the application, since the artifact, the
command, the environment and the credentials a process was handed are
all decided when it starts.
.SH IDENTITY
Every application runs as an Euclid identity, and signs the calls it
makes back into Euclid with that identity's access key - so what an
application does is attributable to it and authorized like anything
else.
.PP
Unless
.B create-application
is given
.BR \-\-user ,
that identity is a
.I technical principal
created for the application alone, named \fIapp\-<application\-id>\fR. It
has no password and cannot log in - see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
- and holds nothing but one access key and a grant on the account the
application belongs to. It is deleted with the application, so no
credential outlives what it was issued to, and no application ever has to
run on a person's credentials.
.PP
Naming
.B \-\-buckets
or
.B \-\-queues
at creation narrows it further, to exactly those resources: the storage
and queueing modules refuse anything else that principal asks for, so a
compromised application reaches what it was deployed with and no more.
An application that names neither keeps account-wide access.
.PP
That key never reaches the application. What the manager hands each process is a short-lived
bearer token, in the file named by
.IR EUCLID_CREDENTIALS_FILE ,
alongside
.IR EUCLID_ENDPOINT ,
.IR EUCLID_ACCOUNT_ID ,
.I EUCLID_REGION
and
.IR EUCLID_USER_ID .
The token is good for
.I euclid.modules.eap.credentials-ttl-seconds
(an hour by default) and the manager rewrites the file once less than half
of that remains, so an application must read it when it needs it rather
than remember the first one it saw. Nothing long-lived is left in the
process for anyone to find.
.PP
An application deployed with
.B \-\-user
is the exception: that user's key is its operator's to manage, so it is
passed in
.I EUCLID_ACCESS_KEY_ID
and
.I EUCLID_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
as before, for signing with RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures.
.PP
All
.B eap
commands require administrator privileges; see
.B AUTHENTICATION
below.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-application
Defines a new application from an artifact in a bucket. The application
is created stopped. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1).
.TP
.B update-application
Changes an existing application's definition; only the options given are
altered. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1).
.TP
.B list-applications
Lists the defined applications with their desired state and how many
instances are running. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-applications (1).
.TP
.B get-application
Shows one application's full definition. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1).
.TP
.B delete-application
Deletes an application definition, stopping it if it is running. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-application (1).
.TP
.B start-application
Marks an application as one that should be running. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1).
.TP
.B stop-application
Marks an application as one that should not be running, keeping its
definition. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-stop-application (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I eap
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty " " \fIBOOL\fR
Pretty-print the JSON response. Defaults to \fItrue\fR.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme used for signed service calls: \fIsigv4\fR (the
default) or \fIrfc9421\fR for RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures.
Overrides
.I euclid.cli.signature
from the configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B eap
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
.PP
Every action is administrator-only, since each one decides which code
Euclid executes and under whose identity it runs. The CLI checks this
before sending a request, to fail with a clearer message, but the server
checks independently on every request and is the actual boundary.
.PP
Requesting help for an action does not require a session, so
.B "euclid-cli eap <action> \-\-help"
works before logging in.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, the request could not be sent, or the server rejected
it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or SigV4
access key, created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B eap
action.
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/data/application/<application-id>
Where the manager materialises an application's artifact and starts it.
The directory is the process's working directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Upload an artifact, define an application from it, and start it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket apps --key inbox.jar --file target/inbox.jar

euclid-cli eap create-application \e
    --application-id inbox --runtime JAVA \e
    --bucket apps --artifact inbox.jar \e
    --min-instances 1 --max-instances 4 --ready-timeout 30000

euclid-cli eap start-application --application-id inbox
euclid-cli eap list-applications
.fi
.RE
.PP
Deploy a new build of the same application:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket apps --key inbox.jar --file target/inbox.jar
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --artifact inbox.jar
.fi
.RE
